msgid "Funqy Google Cloud Functions"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The guide walks through quickstart code to show you how you can deploy Funqy "
"functions to Google Cloud Functions."
msgstr ""

msgid "This technology is considered {extension-status}."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In _preview_, backward compatibility and presence in the ecosystem is not "
"guaranteed.\n"
"Specific improvements might require changing configuration or APIs, and "
"plans to become _stable_ are under way.\n"
"Feedback is welcome on our https://groups.google.com/d/forum/quarkus-"
"dev[mailing list] or as issues in our https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/"
"issues[GitHub issue tracker]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For a full list of possible statuses, check our https://quarkus.io/faq/#what-"
"are-the-extension-statuses[FAQ entry]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

msgid "To complete this guide, you need:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Roughly {prerequisites-time}"
msgstr ""

msgid "An IDE"
msgstr ""

msgid "JDK 17+ installed with `JAVA_HOME` configured appropriately"
msgstr ""

msgid "Apache Maven {maven-version}"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Optionally the xref:cli-tooling.adoc[Quarkus CLI] if you want to use it"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"https://cloud.google.com/[A Google Cloud Account].  Free accounts work."
msgstr ""

msgid "https://cloud.google.com/sdk[Cloud SDK CLI Installed]"
msgstr ""

msgid "Login to Google Cloud"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Login to Google Cloud is necessary for deploying the application. It can be "
"done as follows:"
msgstr ""

msgid "The Quickstart"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Clone the Git repository: `git clone {quickstarts-clone-url}`, or download "
"an {quickstarts-archive-url}[archive]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The solution is located in the `funqy-google-cloud-functions-quickstart` "
"link:{quickstarts-tree-url}/funqy-quickstarts/funqy-google-cloud-functions-"
"quickstart[directory]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Creating the Maven Deployment Project"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Create an application with the `quarkus-funqy-google-cloud-functions` "
"extension.\n"
"You can use the following Maven command to create it:"
msgstr ""

msgid "CLI"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To create a Gradle project, add the `--gradle` or `--gradle-kotlin-dsl` "
"option."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For more information about how to install and use the Quarkus CLI, see the "
"xref:cli-tooling.adoc[Quarkus CLI] guide."
msgstr ""

msgid "Maven"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To create a Gradle project, add the `-DbuildTool=gradle` or `-DbuildTool="
"gradle-kotlin-dsl` option."
msgstr ""

msgid "For Windows users:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If using cmd, (don't use backward slash `\\` and put everything on the same "
"line)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If using Powershell, wrap `-D` parameters in double quotes e.g. `\"-"
"DprojectArtifactId={create-app-artifact-id}\"`"
msgstr ""

msgid "The Code"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"There is nothing special about the code and more importantly nothing Google "
"Cloud specific.  Funqy functions can be deployed to many\n"
"environments and Google Cloud Functions is one of them."
msgstr ""

msgid "Choose Your Function"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Only one Funqy function can be exported per Google Cloud Functions "
"deployment.  If you only have one method\n"
"annotated with `@Funq` in your project, then there is no worries.  If you "
"have multiple functions defined\n"
"within your project, then you will need to choose the function within your "
"Quarkus `application.properties`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can set the `QUARKUS_FUNQY_EXPORT` environment variable "
"when you create the Google Cloud Function using the `gcloud` cli."
msgstr ""

msgid "Build and Deploy"
msgstr ""

msgid "Build the project:"
msgstr ""

msgid "This will compile and package your code."
msgstr ""

msgid "Creating the functions"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In this example, we will create two background functions and a cloud events "
"function.\n"
"Background functions allow you to react to Google Cloud events like PubSub "
"messages, Cloud Storage events, Firestore events, ...\n"
"Cloud events functions allow you to react to supported events using the "
"Cloud Events specification."
msgstr ""

msgid "Injection works inside your function."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is a background function that takes as parameter a `io.quarkus.funqy."
"gcp.functions.event.PubsubMessage`,\n"
"this is a convenient class to deserialize a PubSub message."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is a background function that takes as parameter a `io.quarkus.funqy."
"gcp.functions.event.StorageEvent`,\n"
"this is a convenient class to deserialize a Google Storage event."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is a cloud events function, that takes as parameter a `io.cloudevents."
"CloudEvent`,\n"
"inside it the `getData()` method will return the event content, a storage "
"event in this case."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As our project contains multiple functions, we need to specify which "
"function needs to be deployed via the following property inside our "
"`application.properties` :"
msgstr ""

msgid "Build and Deploy to Google Cloud"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To build your application, you can package your application via `mvn clean "
"package`.\n"
"You will have a single JAR inside the `target/deployment` repository that "
"contains your classes and all your dependencies in it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Then you will be able to use `gcloud` to deploy your function to Google "
"Cloud.\n"
"The `gcloud` command will be different depending on which event triggers "
"your function."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The first time you launch the `gcloud functions deploy`, you can have the "
"following error message:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This means that Cloud Build is not activated yet. To overcome this error, "
"open the URL shown in the error, follow the instructions and then wait a few "
"minutes before retrying the command."
msgstr ""

msgid "Background Functions - PubSub"
msgstr ""

msgid "Use this command to deploy to Google Cloud Functions:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The entry point always needs to be `io.quarkus.funqy.gcp.functions."
"FunqyBackgroundFunction` as it will be this class\n"
"that will bootstrap Quarkus."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `--trigger-resource` option defines the name of the PubSub topic, and "
"the `--trigger-event google.pubsub.topic.publish` option\n"
"define that this function will be triggered by all message publication "
"inside the topic."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To trigger an event to this function, you can use the `gcloud functions "
"call` command:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `--data '{\"data\":\"Hello, Pub/Sub\"}'` option allows you to specify "
"the message to be sent to PubSub."
msgstr ""

msgid "Background Functions - Cloud Storage"
msgstr ""

msgid "Before deploying your function, you need to create a bucket."
msgstr ""

msgid "Then, use this command to deploy to Google Cloud Functions:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `--trigger-resource` option defines the name of the Cloud Storage "
"bucket, and the `--trigger-event google.storage.object.finalize` option\n"
"define that this function will be triggered by all new file inside this "
"bucket."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `--data '{\"name\":\"test.txt\"}'` option allow to specify a fake file "
"name, a fake Cloud Storage event will be created for this name."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can also simply add a file to Cloud Storage using the command line of "
"the web console."
msgstr ""

msgid "Cloud Events Functions - Cloud Storage"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The entry point always needs to be `io.quarkus.funqy.gcp.functions."
"FunqyCloudEventsFunction` as it will be this class\n"
"that will bootstrap Quarkus."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `--trigger-bucket=` option defines the name of the Cloud Storage bucket."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To trigger the event, you can send a file to the GCS `example-cloud-event` "
"bucket."
msgstr ""

msgid "Running locally"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The easiest way to locally run your function is using the Cloud Function "
"invoker JAR."
msgstr ""

msgid "You can download it via Maven using the following command:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Before using the invoker, you first need to build your function via:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Then you can use it to launch your function locally, again, the command "
"depends on the type of function and the type of events."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For background functions, you launch the invoker with a target class of `io."
"quarkus.funqy.gcp.functions.FunqyBackgroundFunction`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Then you can call your background function via an HTTP call with a payload "
"containing the event:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This will call your PubSub background function with a PubSubMessage "
"`{\"data\":\"hello\"}`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This will call your PubSub background function with a Cloud Storage event "
"`{\"name\":\"file.txt\"}`, so an event on the `file.txt` file."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For cloud events functions, you launch the invoker with a target class of "
"`io.quarkus.funqy.gcp.functions.FunqyCloudEventsFunction``."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This will call your cloud events function with an event on the `\"MY_FILE."
"txt` file."
msgstr ""

msgid "Testing your function"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus provides built-in support for testing your Funqy Google Cloud "
"functions via the `quarkus-test-google-cloud-functions` dependency."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To use it, you must add the following test dependencies in your `pom.xml`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This extension provides a `@WithFunction` annotation that can be used to "
"annotate `@QuarkusTest` test cases to start a Cloud Function invoker before "
"you test cases and stop it at the end.\n"
"This annotation must be configured with the type of the function you want to "
"launch, and optionally the name of the function in case you have multiple "
"functions inside your application."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The default Quarkus test port configuration (`quarkus.http.test-port`) will "
"be honored and if you set it to 0 a random port will be assigned to the "
"function invoker."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is a standard Quarkus test that must be annotated by `@QuarkusTest`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`@WithFunction(FunctionType.FUNQY_BACKGROUND)` indicates to launch the "
"function as a Funqy background function. If multiple functions exist in the "
"same application, the `functionName` attribute must be used to denote which "
"one should be launched."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"REST-assured is used to test the function, `{\"data\":\"world\"}` will be "
"sent to it via the invoker."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"REST-assured is used to test the function, `{\"name\":\"hello.txt\"}` will "
"be sent to it via the invoker."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`@WithFunction(FunctionType.FUNQY_CLOUD_EVENTS)` indicates to launch the "
"function as a Funqy cloud events function. If multiple functions exist in "
"the same application, the `functionName` attribute must be used to denote "
"which one should be launched."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"REST-assured is used to test the function, this payload that describe a "
"storage event will be sent to it via the invoker."
msgstr ""

msgid "The cloud events headers must be sent via HTTP headers."
msgstr ""

msgid "What&#8217;s next?"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you are looking for Jakarta REST, Servlet or Vert.x support for Google "
"Cloud Functions, we have it thanks to our xref:gcp-functions-http."
"adoc[Google Cloud Functions HTTP binding]."
msgstr ""
